Subject: [#94-4-25] left anarchists and property

Dan Davis (buy buy) [#94-4-8]--
> A while back Mr. Sherwood posted that in his discussions with
> left anarchists, he found they opposed right anarchists because
> the lefties considered property in land undesirable. I was
> wondering if they opposed all forms of property, or only certain
> types, and if any of them oppose only certain types of property,
> what grounds do they use to maintain the distinction? 

It's hard to sort them out.  Some say personal property is 
redundant in anarchy, because whatever you "need" is produced in 
abundance and nobody keeps it from you -- which is as frustrating 
to us as "the market will take care of it" is to the left.  Some 
say that since all tangible property originates partly in land, 
property is, well, theft.  

Some seem to say property is okay so long as it's not a means of 
production, but I can't pin anyone down on that (let alone get 
them to identify a dividing line); and some say even that is okay 
so long as it's not used for exploitation (i.e. no wage-workers).  
So I harp on the idea that capitalism can't be prevented as long 
as people are free to own things and exchange them, and attack 
them for denying me the freedom to work for wages.

> When the free marketeers call themselves capitalists, they
> have of course just identified themselves as the enemy of
> the working class . . .

That problem is not entirely of the lefties' making; it's not 
that "capitalist" has two meanings, embracing me, a propertyless
wage-worker with capitalist ideology, and Ross Perot, a fascist 
whose job-description happens to be capitalist.  (It's as if 
"democrat" had the alternate meaning of "bureaucrat".)  
"Propertarian" avoids the problem.

