position (a knee-jerk reaction against dualism), that I don't think
anyone now takes seriously.  (Actually, at the end of my other post, I did
agree that any theory has to ultimately be "mechanistic".)

>Anyway,
>the underlined phrase zeros in on why your proposed "either-or" is flawed.
>You have already assumed that automata can not be conscious which is
>not proven (you can't prove it by philosophy alone) However, in
>a previous paragraph you talked about nondeterminism being a useful part of 
