Further on, I did admit that the 2) option is a possibility, where the
"mechanists" concede that we do enjoy subjective phenomenal qualities or
first person inner feelings, but these have no effects on behaviour.  In
such a possibility, I'm just an automaton.  I'm conscious, therefore
automata can be conscious.

>[much deleted. I don't understand how you are defining consciousness.
>"heat differences?" never heard that one before]

I'm taking consciousness to be the experiencing of first person phenomenal
qualities / qualia or inner feelings, e.g. the state of perceiving a "red"
object, or feeling pain, etc.  If an automaton, creature, or whatever, is
conscious, then there is actually someone "inside" looking out at the
world.  (Assume a video camera was switched on, aimed at a field of long
grass.  Would anyone claim that the camera has first person phenomenal
qualities, and experiences the same perceiving of "green" that we do?)

In order to prove that conscious minds can definitely run on a UTM, there
are three possible conditions, all of which must be proven impossible,
beyond the slightest doubt.  The slightest grain of truth in any one of
them means that the simulation is not accurate, and gradually blows apart
the whole idea if there is any substance to any of them:

1)  The brain has evolved to act as a quantum analog computer, making
    at least _some_ use of the massive parallelism of other worlds.

2)  The conscious mind has some mechanism for selecting one world rather
    than another, endowing subjective free will with at least _some_
    objective reality.

3)  Sentience and experiencing of first person qualia require something,
    anything, physically, in addition to a succession of logical states.


Taking 1) first, here's an example of a proof that children can see and
understand, but computers can't.  We can prove to ourselves that
A * B = B * A for any value of A and B.  Just rotate the rectangle by
90 degrees, and it's obvious that the number of characters stays constant
whatever the size.

    *  *  *  *                   *  *  *
    *  *  *  *  A            B   *  *  *
    *  *  *  *                   *  *  *
         B                       *  *  *
                                    A

A computer would keep on and on trying to falsify this.  It could become
increasingly convinced that the rule was more and more likely to be true,
but the poor computer could never be certain!

You mentioned the limitations of the Bekenstein bound, and we know that
space can't be divided indefinitely into infinitesimal bits, so the
Bekenstein bound would appear to apply to a finite volume of space.
However, single universe models must be rejected unless we revert to
mysticism and/or dualism.  Any scientific theory has to admit the existence
of parallel worlds.  Now, you could argue that quantum mechanics doesn't
require the existence of an infinite number of worlds, only a _very_ large
number, but if the brain could make use of this wouldn't you say a UTM
would be an incomplete simulation? 

As for 2), it's admittedly hard to come up with a credible mechanism,
but the mechanism doesn't need to have the mind acting on matter in any
given world.  It only needs to influence the subjective probability of
ending up in one world rather than another.  So it's an emergent phenomenon
which may only be seen on the level of a very large number of parallel
worlds (a multiverse) just like you can't view consciousness or a weather
system by looking at a single atom.  (Similarly, in a given world, neither
consciousness nor weather systems act on a single atom.)  There's room for
all sorts of middle ground, e.g. "free will" is mostly an illusion, but
does have some reality.

If 2) was true but 1) and 3) were false, say, you might still get a
reasonable simulation running on computers, after possibly adding some
quantum randomness to the machine.

For 3) to be false requires that the succession of logical states (e.g. in
the water pipes, someone turning the pages of a great many books, or being
very productive with chalk and duster) is absolutely all you need in
order for all the properties of consciousness -- all the inner qualia and
range of feelings -- to be satisfactorily reproduced.
 
If 3) was at all true but 1) and 2) were false, there could be other
examples of life (far enough away that we haven't seen their probes yet)
throughout the universe, with some species evolved with the structures of
unconscious automata, whereas we're fortunate enough to be conscious
automata.

>> Ironically, once you move too far from the identity theory of tying up
