>the same one which prevents you from imagining that microscopic
>phenomena are responsible for macroscopic thermodynamics,

I'm not saying that the microscopic events aren't responsible, just that
the image of the single universe is insufficient to get the whole picture,
particularly when you're dealing with something of the complexity of a
human being.  This may be significant, or if we really are just algorithms,
maybe not.

>or the same
>one which stops creationists from imagining how evolution could have
>produced beings as fantastically complex as us. The error is that
>seemingly simple phenonmena cannot lead *in combination* over time or space
>to very complex phenomena. (i.e. that any combination of algorithms
>could produce something as complex as the mind)

This helps to show how complex algorithms could evolve by starting with
a very simple algorithm, then adding others and building up until a very
complex computational device arises.  Just like we know we've evolved to
become more and more complex in terms of physical structure.  It doesn't
prove that algorithms alone are sufficient for inner mental states.  I
like the idea of a von Neumannesque virtual serial machine evolving on top
of a parallel architecture, but this does seem to suggest that it evolved
for a purpose.  Possibly to make it easier for the "users" to reprogram
themselves, after reflecting on past mistakes or successes.

>Also, logical states and 
>physical states are convertable (give me a physical state, and I'll
>give you a machine with the same logical state, or I'll convert a
>logical state into a physical device representing that logical state)

The logical states could be embodied from a wide range of physical devices,
and this lack of a direct tie up between mental states and physical states
seems to lead to an oddity regarding our arrow of time.  If consciousness
is solely a series of logical states, how does it 'know' which way to run?
(Brings to mind: "The pedestrian didn't know which way to run, so I ran
over him".)  And if you had a Chinese room designed to simulate two
individuals, operated by two teams using color coded blackboards and look-
up tables, wouldn't the two minds get mixed up over who was who?  Maybe
there would only be one possible coherent interpretation of all these
logical states, in both examples.  Other interpretations would be too
jumbled to produce a mind -- if they did, it would be a split personality. 

>> In my alternative, each possible mental state, however complex, could
>> be represented by its own particular landscape involving the fifth
>> dimension of electromagnetic space.  [...]
>
>Nice theory (sounds almost like Star Trek technobabble), but Fifth dimension?
>Doesn't exist except in Kaluza-Klien models which aren't generally accepted. 

Kaluza's 1921 model was a perfect unification of electromagnetism and
gravity, and successfully replaced the obsolete idea of "force fields" with
geometric explanations in both domains.  In any case, if other dimensions
aren't accepted then concepts such as cosmic inflation are reduced to
nothing more than Star Trek technobabble.  

>> If the universe is a simulation then our consciousness is likely just a
>> succession of logical states.  But then it might be said that the simulated
>> beings were already simulations, therefore they hadn't successfully 
>> simulated themselves even if they thought they had -- it was an illusion.  
>
>   Why do you believe a simulation of a simulation is not a simulation,
>or that simulations are illusions?

Here I was imagining the situation where the simulators' machines don't
have sufficient computing power to properly cope with simulated beings
simulating themselves, and instead use short cuts.  The simulated
universe is supposed to operate according to a certain set of laws, and
its beings don't find any fuzzy anomalies until they make observations at
the sub-atomic level.  Let's say they were allowed to start trying to
simulate themselves by modelling particles, etc, and the simulators'
machines can't resolve to these depths.  So the simulators cheat and
fabricate things to make the simulations think they've simulated
themselves.  D'you think the simulators' machines could be so powerful as
to allow simulated beings to successfully simulate themselves?

Actually, if the universe is simulated then Turing machine theories could
be a hoax.  All they need to do is tamper with our minds to make us think
a UTM can do any algorithmic procedure, then when we try to simulate minds
with UTMs the simulators fall about laughing for a while, then power down
the machines and revise the software ready for the next run.  

>You are making arguments by assuming the conclusion.

I'm willing to assume all sorts of premises or conclusions and look at
the implications.  I don't think it can be proven either way whether a
UTM could successfully simulate a mind (after allowances for speed);
certainly it's quite possible.  I'd give it about 0.1 probability, and
10^-20 for the universe being a simulation.

The 10^-20 figure is one I assigned to "deathism is an extropic strategy
which works to the benefit of the individual practising it".  If you 
start raising the simulated universe figure, the probabilities of
reincarnation being true and deathism being extropic would seem to rise,
since the simulators might save personalities and resurrect them into a
better life! 

