And now, it's time for Uncle Grigsby's Practical Philosophy Korner:

Q: What is a right?
A: An expectation of others' behavior.

Q: What is a moral?
A: A justification for demanding a right.

If a state exists, you can add onto these answers "and goons will whack
you on the head if you don't conform to it."  If a state does not exist,
there is no concrete difference at all between rights and politeness,
merely a degree of uncomfortableness with said behavior.

> The purpose of private property is not just privacy -- it's the 
> ability to *do stuff* without collective permission.  You can't 
> experiment on crops if The People in their wisdom decide that 
> your experiments aren't worth the land it takes to do them.

In the absence of the State, this distinction is worthless, and the whole
private vs. public property debate becomes much less important.  All this
talk about "doing stuff without permission" implies that there exists some
authority that can grant or revoke this permission.  In anarchy, your claim
to private property is only as solid as your willingness to defend it,
and the concept of a "right" devolves onto the above definition.

// g

